    old_value     text null
    new_value     text null
    occurred_at   timestamptz not null
    index on (task_id, id), index on (id)       -- the second is sub-project 3's cursor

  [1mThree notes on the choices:[0m

  - [1m[38;5;153mstatus[39m as an enum[0m — [38;5;153m'todo' | 'in_progress' | 'done'[39m. Three states is enough to be useful and makes "moved to done" a
    clean event. More states are a migration, not a redesign.
  - [1mOne row per changed field, not per request.[0m Editing title and status together writes two [38;5;153mtask_events[39m rows. This
    costs a little storage but means sub-project 3 can say "status changed to done" without diffing JSON blobs, and lets
    users subscribe per field later.
  - [1m[38;5;153mactor_id[39m is recorded[0m specifically so notifications can suppress self-notification — you don't want an email about
    your own edit. Capturing it now is free; adding it later means the early events can't be filtered.

  [1mThe one open decision in this section: task visibility.[0m The schema above has no scoping column, which means every task
  is visible and assignable to every user — a single shared workspace. That's fine for a small team and it's the
  simplest thing that satisfies your request. The alternative is adding a [38;5;153mprojects[39m (or [38;5;153mworkspaces[39m) table with
  membership, which makes "tasks they care about" partly a [3mmembership[0m question and adds an authorization check to every
  query. Adding scoping later is a real migration plus an audit of every read path, so it's worth deciding now rather
  than assuming.

  I'd recommend the flat single workspace for v1: your request is about [3mchange[0m notifications, not access control, and a
  [38;5;153mprojects[39m table would expand sub-project 2 considerably.
